{"format":"bench-library","formatVersion":1,"id":"lcd-i2c","name":"LCD 16x2 and 20x4 (I2C)","version":"1.1.0","author":"Bench Workshop","description":"The classic blue 16x2 character LCD and its bigger 20x4 brother (HD44780) with an I2C backpack (PCF8574): text over just 2 wires. Includes LiquidCrystal_I2C.h for sketches; real firmware using LiquidCrystal_I2C works too.","category":"Displays","code":"// HD44780 character LCD (16x2 or 20x4) behind a PCF8574 I2C backpack. Every byte written to the PCF8574 sets\r\n// its 8 outputs: P0 = RS, P1 = RW, P2 = E, P3 = backlight, P4-P7 = the LCD's D4-D7. The LCD takes\r\n// D4-D7 when E falls. It starts in 8-bit mode (each E pulse = one whole command, only the top four\r\n// bits seen) until a \"function set\" switches it to 4-bit mode (two E pulses per byte, high half first).\r\nbench.device('lcd1602', (part) => {\r\n  const screen = part.screen('lcd');\r\n  const addr = Number(part.params.addr ?? 0x27);\r\n  const ddram = new Array(128).fill(0x20);\r\n  let ac = 0, inc = true, displayOn = false, shift = 0, fourBit = false, half = null, port = 0, powered = false, writes = 0, cgram = false;\r\n\r\n  const reset = () => {\r\n    ddram.fill(0x20);\r\n    ac = 0; inc = true; displayOn = false; shift = 0; fourBit = false; half = null; port = 0; cgram = false;\r\n  };\r\n  const command = (c) => {\r\n    if (c & 0x80) { ac = c & 0x7f; cgram = false; }\r\n    else if (c & 0x40) cgram = true; // custom characters: kept out of the picture\r\n    else if (c & 0x20) { fourBit = !(c & 0x10); half = null; }\r\n    else if (c & 0x10) { if (!(c & 0x08)) ac = (ac + ((c & 0x04) ? 1 : -1)) & 0x7f; else shift += (c & 0x04) ? 1 : -1; }\r\n    else if (c & 0x08) displayOn = !!(c & 0x04);\r\n    else if (c & 0x04) inc = !!(c & 0x02);\r\n    else if (c & 0x02) { ac = 0; shift = 0; }\r\n    else if (c & 0x01) { ddram.fill(0x20); ac = 0; shift = 0; inc = true; }\r\n  };\r\n  const data = (d) => {\r\n    if (cgram) return;\r\n    ddram[ac] = d;\r\n    ac = (ac + (inc ? 1 : -1)) & 0x7f;\r\n  };\r\n  const latch = (byte) => {\r\n    const rs = byte & 1, nibble = byte >> 4;\r\n    if (!fourBit) {\r\n      const v = nibble << 4;\r\n      return rs ? data(v) : command(v);\r\n    }\r\n    if (half === null) { half = nibble; return; }\r\n    const v = (half << 4) | nibble;\r\n    half = null;\r\n    rs ? data(v) : command(v);\r\n  };\r\n  const line = (start) => {\r\n    let s = '';\r\n    for (let c = 0; c < screen.cols; c++) {\r\n      const code = ddram[(start + ((c + shift) % 40 + 40) % 40) & 0x7f];\r\n      s += code >= 0x20 && code < 0x7f ? String.fromCharCode(code) : code < 8 ? '█' : '?';\r\n    }\r\n    return s;\r\n  };\r\n\r\n  return {\r\n    i2c: {\r\n      address: () => (powered ? addr : -1),\r\n      write(bytes) {\r\n        for (const b of bytes) {\r\n          writes++;\r\n          if ((port & 0x04) && !(b & 0x04)) latch(port); // E falls: the LCD takes D4-D7 (as they were while E was high)\r\n          port = b & 0xff;\r\n        }\r\n        return true;\r\n      },\r\n      read(n) { return new Array(n).fill(port); },\r\n    },\r\n    step(io) {\r\n      const now = io.supply('VCC', 'GND') >= 2.7;\r\n      if (now !== powered) { powered = now; if (!now) reset(); }\r\n      screen.light(powered && !!(port & 0x08));\r\n      // (lines start at these addresses: 16x2 uses the first two, 20x4 all four)\r\n      [0x00, 0x40, 0x14, 0x54].slice(0, screen.rows).forEach((start, r) => screen.print(0, r, powered && displayOn ? line(start) : ' '.repeat(screen.cols)));\r\n    },\r\n    status() {\r\n      if (!powered) return ['no power'];\r\n      return [`I2C 0x${addr.toString(16).toUpperCase()}, ${displayOn ? 'display on' : 'display off'}, backlight ${port & 0x08 ? 'on' : 'off'}, ${writes} bytes`];\r\n    },\r\n  };\r\n});\r\n","sketch":{"LiquidCrystal_I2C.h":{"classes":["LiquidCrystal_I2C"],"code":"// LiquidCrystal_I2C for sketches: the usual functions (init/begin, backlight, clear, setCursor, print...)\n// driving an HD44780 through a PCF8574 backpack the way the Arduino library does. Each command goes\n// out as one I2C transfer (the PCF8574 takes a stream of bytes, setting its outputs on each one).\nconst LCD_RS = 0x01, LCD_EN = 0x04, LCD_BL = 0x08;\n\nclass LiquidCrystal_I2C {\n  constructor(addr, cols = 16, rows = 2) {\n    this.addr = addr;\n    this.cols = cols;\n    this.rows = rows;\n    this.bl = LCD_BL;\n    this.ctrl = 0x04; // display on, cursor off, blink off\n    this.mode = 0x02; // left to right\n  }\n  send(bytes) {\n    Wire.beginTransmission(this.addr);\n    for (const b of bytes) Wire.write(b);\n    return Wire.endTransmission();\n  }\n  nibbles(value, rs) {\n    const out = [];\n    for (const n of [value & 0xf0, (value << 4) & 0xf0]) out.push(n | rs | this.bl | LCD_EN, n | rs | this.bl);\n    return out;\n  }\n  command(c) { this.send(this.nibbles(c, 0)); }\n  init() { this.begin(this.cols, this.rows); }\n  begin(cols = this.cols, rows = this.rows) {\n    this.cols = cols;\n    this.rows = rows;\n    Wire.begin();\n    delay(50);\n    this.send([this.bl]);\n    // the HD44780's wake-up: three times \"8-bit mode\", then \"4-bit mode\"\n    for (const n of [0x30, 0x30, 0x30, 0x20]) {\n      this.send([n | this.bl | LCD_EN, n | this.bl]);\n      delay(5);\n    }\n    this.command(0x20 | (rows > 1 ? 0x08 : 0)); // 4-bit, lines, 5x8 dots\n    this.display();\n    this.clear();\n    this.command(0x04 | this.mode);\n    this.home();\n  }\n  clear() { this.command(0x01); delay(2); }\n  home() { this.command(0x02); delay(2); }\n  setCursor(col, row) {\n    const offsets = [0x00, 0x40, 0x14, 0x54];\n    this.command(0x80 | (col + offsets[Math.min(row, this.rows - 1, 3)]));\n  }\n  display() { this.ctrl |= 0x04; this.command(0x08 | this.ctrl); }\n  noDisplay() { this.ctrl &= ~0x04; this.command(0x08 | this.ctrl); }\n  cursor() { this.ctrl |= 0x02; this.command(0x08 | this.ctrl); }\n  noCursor() { this.ctrl &= ~0x02; this.command(0x08 | this.ctrl); }\n  blink() { this.ctrl |= 0x01; this.command(0x08 | this.ctrl); }\n  noBlink() { this.ctrl &= ~0x01; this.command(0x08 | this.ctrl); }\n  scrollDisplayLeft() { this.command(0x18); }\n  scrollDisplayRight() { this.command(0x1c); }\n  backlight() { this.bl = LCD_BL; this.send([this.bl]); }\n  noBacklight() { this.bl = 0; this.send([0]); }\n  setBacklight(on) { on ? this.backlight() : this.noBacklight(); }\n  createChar(location, charmap) {\n    this.command(0x40 | ((location & 7) << 3));\n    this.send(charmap.slice(0, 8).flatMap((b) => this.nibbles(b, LCD_RS)));\n  }\n  write(b) { this.send(this.nibbles(Number(b) & 0xff, LCD_RS)); return 1; }\n  print(x, f) {\n    let s;\n    if (typeof x === 'number' && f !== undefined) s = f === 16 ? x.toString(16).toUpperCase() : f === 2 ? x.toString(2) : x.toFixed(f);\n    else if (typeof x === 'number' && !Number.isInteger(x)) s = x.toFixed(2);\n    else s = String(x);\n    const bytes = [];\n    for (const ch of s) bytes.push(...this.nibbles(ch.charCodeAt(0) & 0xff, LCD_RS));\n    if (bytes.length) this.send(bytes);\n    return s.length;\n  }\n  println(x, f) { return this.print(x, f); }\n  printf(fmt, ...a) {\n    return this.print(fmt.replace(/%(-?\\d*)(?:\\.(\\d+))?([dfsxXc%])/g, (_m, w, p, t) => {\n      if (t === '%') return '%';\n      const v = a.shift();\n      let s = t === 'f' ? Number(v).toFixed(p ? Number(p) : 2) : t === 'x' ? Number(v).toString(16) : t === 'X' ? Number(v).toString(16).toUpperCase() : t === 'c' ? String.fromCharCode(v) : t === 'd' ? String(Math.trunc(v)) : String(v);\n      const width = Math.abs(Number(w || 0));\n      if (s.length < width) s = w.startsWith('-') ? s.padEnd(width) : s.padStart(width, w.startsWith('0') ? '0' : ' ');\n      return s;\n    }));\n  }\n}\n"}},"parts":[{"id":"lcd1602","name":"LCD 16x2 (I2C)","description":"16x2 character display on an I2C backpack (address 0x27, or 0x3F on some). VCC to 5 V, GND, SDA, SCL. In a sketch: #include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h>, LiquidCrystal_I2C lcd(0x27, 16, 2); lcd.init(); lcd.backlight(); lcd.print(\"Hello\");","swatch":"#3a6fe0","mass":35,"params":[{"key":"addr","label":"I2C address","kind":"select","options":[{"value":"39","label":"0x27"},{"value":"63","label":"0x3F"}]}],"defaults":{"addr":"39"},"shape":[{"box":[8,0.16,3.6],"at":[0,0.08,0],"color":"#1d6b3a"},{"box":[7.1,0.7,2.4],"at":[0,0.51,0],"color":"#1a1a1c"},{"screen":{"id":"lcd","size":[6.4,1.45],"px":[16,2],"mode":"text","on":"#eef4ff","off":"#13204a","bg":"#3a6fe0"},"at":[0,0.862,0]},{"box":[1.9,0.3,4.1],"at":[-3.5,-0.15,0],"color":"#202a24","collide":false}],"pinRows":[{"ids":["GND","VCC","SDA","SCL"],"at":[-4.2,0,-0.381],"dir":"z","kinds":{"GND":"gnd","VCC":"vin","SDA":"i2c","SCL":"i2c"},"extra":{"VCC":{"label":"VCC (5 V)","vmin":2.7,"vmax":5.5}}}],"device":"lcd1602"},{"id":"lcd2004","name":"LCD 20x4 (I2C)","description":"20x4 character display on an I2C backpack (address 0x27, or 0x3F on some). VCC to 5 V, GND, SDA, SCL. In a sketch: #include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h>, LiquidCrystal_I2C lcd(0x27, 20, 4); lcd.init(); lcd.backlight(); lcd.print(\"Hello\");","swatch":"#3a6fe0","mass":60,"params":[{"key":"addr","label":"I2C address","kind":"select","options":[{"value":"39","label":"0x27"},{"value":"63","label":"0x3F"}]}],"defaults":{"addr":"39"},"shape":[{"box":[9.8,0.16,6],"at":[0,0.08,0],"color":"#1d6b3a"},{"box":[9.7,0.7,4],"at":[0,0.51,0],"color":"#1a1a1c"},{"screen":{"id":"lcd","size":[7.7,2.6],"px":[20,4],"mode":"text","on":"#eef4ff","off":"#13204a","bg":"#3a6fe0"},"at":[0,0.862,0]},{"box":[1.9,0.3,4.1],"at":[-4.3,-0.15,0],"color":"#202a24","collide":false}],"pinRows":[{"ids":["GND","VCC","SDA","SCL"],"at":[-5,0,-0.381],"dir":"z","kinds":{"GND":"gnd","VCC":"vin","SDA":"i2c","SCL":"i2c"},"extra":{"VCC":{"label":"VCC (5 V)","vmin":2.7,"vmax":5.5}}}],"device":"lcd1602"}],"owner":"codeopen0-cpu"}